id: design.schema-migration
kind: design
title: Schema migration compatibility
aliases: migration, schema change, expand contract, database migration
tags: database, deployment, reliability
questions:
  - how do you deploy a database change with zero downtime?
invariant: Every schema change must be compatible with both the old and the new version of the application code while both are running.
answer_30s: Use expand/contract: add the new column or table, deploy code that writes both and reads either, backfill, switch reads, then remove the old shape in a later deploy.
answer_2m: |
  The reason is the rolling update: at some point during a rollout, old and new
  code run simultaneously against the same schema, so any change that breaks one
  of them breaks the deployment. Expand/contract turns one risky change into
  several safe ones, each independently deployable and rollback-safe. Practical
  rules: additive changes first (new nullable column, new table, new index
  concurrently), never rename or drop in the same release as the code change,
  avoid long locks (add indexes CONCURRENTLY, batch backfills, avoid table
  rewrites), and keep migrations idempotent and forward-only. Backfills are the
  part that needs design: batched, throttled, resumable, and monitored, because
  a naive UPDATE on a large table locks or saturates the database.
when_to_use:
  - any schema change on a system that deploys without downtime
minimal_example: |
  1. deploy: add column nullable            (old code unaffected)
  2. deploy: write both, read old           (both versions safe)
  3. backfill in batches (resumable)
  4. deploy: read new, still write both
  5. deploy: write new only
  6. later:  drop old column/footprint
failure_modes:
  - renaming a column in one release, so the rollout breaks the old pods
  - a migration that takes an exclusive lock on a hot table
  - one big backfill transaction, blocking writes and generating a huge WAL burst
  - rollback planned without considering that the migration already applied
follow_ups:
  - how does rollback work half way through this sequence?
related:
  - k8s.deployment.rolling-update
  - k8s.job
  - design.idempotency
  - design.state-machine
sources:
  - https://martinfowler.com/articles/evodb.html
  - https://wiki.postgresql.org/wiki/Lock_Monitoring
